<p>Contractors often buy for several projects at once. A job keeps each project's purchases together, so they (and you) can see what it has
  cost, and they can pass the costs on to their own client.</p>

<h2>Adding a job</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open the customer and press <strong>Add a job</strong>, or go to <strong>Sales › Jobs</strong>.</li>
  <li>Give it a name ("Mwangi Towers"), their reference or LPO number if they use one, the site and, if they like, a budget for materials.</li>
</ol>

<h2>At the till</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Choose the customer as usual. If they have open jobs, a <strong>Job</strong> list appears under their name; pick the one they're buying for.</li>
  <li>The till shows how much of the budget is left. Going over is allowed, but it shows in red.</li>
  <li>The job is printed on the receipt and on the A4 tax invoice.</li>
  <li>Quotes and orders can be for a job too (<strong>New quote for this job</strong> on the job's page). When the order is collected, the sale
    is for the same job.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What a job has cost</h2>
<p>The job's page shows what it has cost so far (sales less anything returned), how that compares with the budget, every material that went
  into it, and its sales, returns and open orders. <strong>Cost summary (PDF)</strong> gives the contractor the same on your letterhead.</p>
<p>When the work is finished, press <strong>Close job</strong> so it stops showing at the till. You can reopen it at any time.</p>
